20
00:01:28.049 --> 00:01:28.967
A story:

21
00:01:29.247 --> 00:01:31.785
214 dark stores paid twice.

22
00:01:32.065 --> 00:01:40.855
The nightly store-settlement job computes each dark store's payout (sales minus refunds, commission and GST) and writes it for the bank file.

23
00:01:41.135 --> 00:01:46.639
It must run on exactly one pod, so the team used a Redis lock with a 10-second TTL:

24
00:01:46.919 --> 00:01:50.012
SET settle-lock pod-A NX PX 10000.

25
00:01:50.292 --> 00:01:52.041
Whoever got the lock was the leader.

26
00:01:53.391 --> 00:01:57.125
One Diwali night, pod A took the lock and loaded the day's sales.

27
00:01:57.405 --> 00:02:05.789
Then it stopped for 14 seconds: a full GC on a heap swollen by the sale-day data, while its VM was also being live-migrated.

28
00:02:06.069 --> 00:02:07.327
The lock expired.

29
00:02:07.607 --> 00:02:14.562
Pod B took it, recomputed every payout (now including 3,100 late refunds) and wrote them.

30
00:02:14.842 --> 00:02:15.887
Pod A woke up.

31
00:02:16.167 --> 00:02:18.535
From its point of view no time had passed.

32
00:02:18.815 --> 00:02:25.855
It still held the lock, so it wrote its payouts over B's, and the bank-file step, triggered by both, picked up both batches.

33
00:02:26.135 --> 00:02:30.765
214 stores were paid twice, 38 lakh rupees in total.

34
00:02:31.045 --> 00:02:33.071
Recovering it took three weeks of calls.

35
00:02:34.421 --> 00:02:39.733
The same week, support was confused by order timelines that showed Packing before Paid.

36
00:02:40.013 --> 00:02:50.872
Events from different services were sorted by their wall-clock timestamps, and the order-service host's clock had drifted 90 ms behind after an NTP misconfiguration.

37
00:02:51.152 --> 00:02:53.157
Neither was a bug in one service.

38
00:02:53.437 --> 00:02:58.621
Both came from assumptions about time and agreement that don't hold in a distributed system:

39
00:02:59.971 --> 00:03:03.235
A lock with a TTL doesn't stop a paused process.

40
00:03:03.515 --> 00:03:05.329
The process doesn't know it paused.

41
00:03:05.609 --> 00:03:15.657
The fix is a fencing token: a number that increases with every new lease, sent with every write, and checked by the resource (update... where fence =:token).

42
00:03:15.937 --> 00:03:19.243
The lab shows both outcomes on real PostgreSQL.

43
00:03:19.523 --> 00:03:35.075
Who is the leader must be decided by a consensus system (etcd, ZooKeeper, a Kubernetes Lease backed by etcd, or a database acting as the single linearizable store), not by a cache that can lose writes on failover (Ch 10).

44
00:03:36.425 --> 00:03:40.095
Wall-clock timestamps from different machines don't order events.

45
00:03:40.375 --> 00:03:44.535
Causally related events need a logical or hybrid logical clock.

46
00:03:44.815 --> 00:03:46.713
Timeouts need a monotonic clock.

47
00:03:48.063 --> 00:03:48.746
Quote:

48
00:03:49.026 --> 00:03:50.690
The thesis of this chapter.

49
00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:58.586
In a distributed system there is no global now and no reliable way to know whether another process is dead or just slow.

50
00:03:58.866 --> 00:04:03.154
Consistency models say exactly which orderings a reader may observe.

51
00:04:03.434 --> 00:04:08.405
Clocks give you orderings that are either causal (logical) or approximate (physical).

52
00:04:08.685 --> 00:04:11.351
Consensus lets a majority agree anyway.

53
00:04:11.631 --> 00:04:21.914
Anything else that claims exactly one leader or latest value is relying on timing assumptions, so make those assumptions explicit and put a fence behind them.

54
