110
00:06:56.383 --> 00:06:58.282
Core Concepts, Beginner to Deep.

111
00:06:59.532 --> 00:07:02.689
Intuition: a family WhatsApp group deciding dinner.

112
00:07:02.969 --> 00:07:06.148
Five family members decide on dinner over WhatsApp.

113
00:07:06.428 --> 00:07:08.775
Messages arrive late and out of order.

114
00:07:09.055 --> 00:07:15.775
Your phone shows Sure, pizza! before Pizza or biryani? because the first message was stuck on a slow network.

115
00:07:16.055 --> 00:07:21.495
Each phone's clock also differs by a few seconds, so timestamps don't settle who said what first.

116
00:07:21.775 --> 00:07:23.012
Someone goes quiet.

117
00:07:23.292 --> 00:07:26.364
Is Uncle offline, asleep, or just typing slowly?

118
00:07:26.644 --> 00:07:27.604
You can't tell.

119
00:07:27.884 --> 00:07:33.751
That's the core problem of distributed systems: a slow process looks exactly like a dead one.

120
00:07:35.101 --> 00:07:39.623
The decision rule: whatever a majority (3 of 5) agrees to is final.

121
00:07:39.903 --> 00:07:46.410
Two majorities always share at least one person, so the family can never decide both pizza and biryani.

122
00:07:46.690 --> 00:07:53.666
If only 2 people are online, no decision is made (the system is unavailable), but it's never inconsistent.

123
00:07:53.946 --> 00:07:55.119
That's consensus.

124
00:07:55.399 --> 00:08:00.349
The rule for the delivery app: only the person holding the family's payment card orders.

125
00:08:00.629 --> 00:08:07.626
If Mom hands the card to Dad while she's stuck in a lift, and then she comes out of the lift and orders anyway, you get two dinners.

126
00:08:07.906 --> 00:08:13.218
Unless the restaurant only accepts orders from the latest card holder: that's a fencing token.

127
00:08:14.568 --> 00:08:17.917
What consistency means (it's used for three different things).

128
00:08:18.197 --> 00:08:19.563
Here is a quick table:

129
00:08:19.843 --> 00:08:21.357
Word, and Meaning, and Where.

130
00:08:21.637 --> 00:08:22.661
C in ACID.

131
00:08:22.941 --> 00:08:23.731
Meaning:

132
00:08:24.011 --> 00:08:27.467
Transactions keep invariants (constraints, business rules).

133
00:08:27.747 --> 00:08:28.408
Where:

134
00:08:28.688 --> 00:08:30.075
Ch 05.

135
00:08:30.355 --> 00:08:32.979
C in CAP or consistency models.

136
00:08:33.259 --> 00:08:34.048
Meaning:

137
00:08:34.328 --> 00:08:41.240
What values reads may return when data is replicated or distributed: linearizable, causal, eventual...

138
00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:42.181
Where:

139
00:08:42.461 --> 00:08:43.464
This chapter.

140
00:08:43.744 --> 00:08:46.325
Isolation (often called consistency).

141
00:08:46.605 --> 00:08:47.395
Meaning:

142
00:08:47.675 --> 00:08:52.560
How concurrent transactions interleave: read committed, snapshot, serializable.

143
00:08:52.840 --> 00:08:53.501
Where:

144
00:08:53.781 --> 00:08:55.168
Ch 05.

145
00:08:56.518 --> 00:09:08.081
Strongly consistent usually means linearizable: the system behaves as if there's one copy of the data, and every operation takes effect at a single instant between its start and its end.

146
00:09:08.361 --> 00:09:15.059
Eventually consistent means replicas converge if writes stop, with no promise about what you read before that.

147
00:09:16.409 --> 00:09:18.095
The consistency-model ladder.

148
00:09:18.375 --> 00:09:22.983
From strongest to weakest (for single objects, plus the transactional versions):

149
00:09:24.333 --> 00:09:25.698
Here is a quick table:

150
00:09:25.978 --> 00:09:28.773
Model, and Guarantee, and Cost, and Example.

151
00:09:29.053 --> 00:09:32.466
Strict serializable (linearizable + serializable).

152
00:09:32.746 --> 00:09:33.727
Guarantee:

153
00:09:34.007 --> 00:09:37.421
Transactions appear in one order that respects real time.

154
00:09:37.701 --> 00:09:38.426
Cost:

155
00:09:38.706 --> 00:09:42.397
Coordination on every transaction, cross-region latency.

156
00:09:42.677 --> 00:09:43.551
Example:

157
00:09:43.831 --> 00:09:50.381
Spanner, CockroachDB (mostly), FoundationDB, single-node PostgreSQL SERIALIZABLE.

158
00:09:50.661 --> 00:09:51.685
Linearizable.

159
00:09:51.965 --> 00:09:52.946
Guarantee:

160
00:09:53.226 --> 00:10:01.247
Single-object ops appear atomic at one instant within their real-time interval; once any read sees a value, all later reads do.

161
00:10:01.527 --> 00:10:02.253
Cost:

162
00:10:02.533 --> 00:10:04.581
A leader or a quorum round per op.

163
00:10:04.861 --> 00:10:09.789
Example: etcd (default reads), ZooKeeper writes, a PostgreSQL primary.

164
00:10:11.139 --> 00:10:12.099
Sequential.

165
00:10:12.379 --> 00:10:13.360
Guarantee:

166
00:10:13.640 --> 00:10:21.149
All clients see the same order of ops, and each client's ops stay in program order, but not necessarily real-time order.

167
00:10:21.429 --> 00:10:22.155
Cost:

168
00:10:22.435 --> 00:10:24.013
Cheaper than linearizable.

169
00:10:24.293 --> 00:10:25.168
Example:

170
00:10:25.448 --> 00:10:27.411
ZooKeeper reads (without sync).

171
00:10:27.691 --> 00:10:28.416
Causal.

172
00:10:28.696 --> 00:10:29.677
Guarantee:

173
00:10:29.957 --> 00:10:35.931
Operations that are causally related are seen in the same order by all; concurrent ones may differ.

174
00:10:36.211 --> 00:10:36.936
Cost:

175
00:10:37.216 --> 00:10:40.672
No global coordination; needs dependency tracking.

176
00:10:40.952 --> 00:10:41.827
Example:

177
00:10:42.107 --> 00:10:46.331
COPS, MongoDB causal sessions, many CRDT systems.

178
00:10:46.611 --> 00:10:47.869
Session guarantees.

179
00:10:48.149 --> 00:10:49.131
Guarantee:

180
00:10:49.411 --> 00:10:54.296
Read-your-writes, monotonic reads, monotonic writes, writes-follow-reads, per client.

181
00:10:54.576 --> 00:10:55.301
Cost:

182
00:10:55.581 --> 00:10:58.035
Session tokens (Ch 10 L2).

183
00:10:58.315 --> 00:10:59.189
Example:

184
00:10:59.469 --> 00:11:00.771
Our replica reads.

185
00:11:02.121 --> 00:11:03.017
Eventual.

186
00:11:03.297 --> 00:11:04.278
Guarantee:

187
00:11:04.558 --> 00:11:06.563
Replicas converge when writes stop.

188
00:11:06.843 --> 00:11:07.569
Cost:

189
00:11:07.849 --> 00:11:09.598
Cheapest, always available.

190
00:11:09.878 --> 00:11:10.753
Example:

191
00:11:11.033 --> 00:11:13.123
DNS, Cassandra ONE, caches.

192
00:11:14.473 --> 00:11:25.481
Linearizability versus serializability: serializability is about transactions (multi-object) appearing in some serial order, which need not match real time.

193
00:11:25.761 --> 00:11:29.943
Linearizability is about single objects respecting real time.

194
00:11:30.223 --> 00:11:32.164
Snapshot isolation is neither.

195
00:11:32.444 --> 00:11:40.295
A read-only replica in a serializable database can still serve stale reads, which is serializable but not linearizable.

196
00:11:40.575 --> 00:11:42.708
Why the distinction matters in practice.

197
00:11:42.988 --> 00:11:45.420
Linearizability is what makes these work:

198
00:11:45.700 --> 00:11:51.332
Leader election and locks: the lock is mine must be the same answer for everyone right now.

199
00:11:52.682 --> 00:11:57.247
Uniqueness constraints: is this username taken? must see the latest write.

200
00:11:57.527 --> 00:12:03.415
Cross-channel ordering: a service writes an image and then sends resize image X through Kafka.

201
00:12:03.695 --> 00:12:07.450
If the resizer reads a stale replica, it doesn't find the image.

202
00:12:07.730 --> 00:12:15.175
L2 checks histories: an async follower read was non-linearizable in 61.67 % of histories.

203
00:12:15.455 --> 00:12:27.850
A quorum read with R + W N, which people often assume is enough, was still non-linearizable in 0.06 %: one client read 2 and a later client read 1.

204
00:12:28.130 --> 00:12:31.927
Only a read that writes back what it read (ABD) got to 0.

205
00:12:33.277 --> 00:12:34.963
CAP and PACELC, precisely.

206
00:12:35.243 --> 00:12:51.328
CAP (Brewer, proven by Gilbert and Lynch): during a network partition, a system that stores replicated data must choose between linearizability (C) and availability (A: every request to a non-failed node gets a non-error response).

207
00:12:51.608 --> 00:12:52.824
Common misreadings:

208
00:12:53.104 --> 00:12:54.533
Pick two of three is wrong.

209
00:12:54.813 --> 00:12:58.675
Partitions aren't optional, so the choice is C or A when partitioned.

210
00:12:58.955 --> 00:13:04.779
The C is linearizability only, and the A is a very strict availability (every node answers).

211
00:13:05.059 --> 00:13:10.008
A CP system is usually still highly available when a majority is reachable.

212
00:13:10.288 --> 00:13:12.635
It just refuses on the minority side.

213
00:13:13.985 --> 00:13:16.715
Most systems are neither fully C nor fully A.

214
00:13:16.995 --> 00:13:24.078
They make per-operation choices (Cassandra consistency levels, etcd serializable versus linearizable reads).

215
00:13:24.358 --> 00:13:31.867
PACELC (Abadi) adds the everyday trade-off: if Partition, choose A or C; Else, choose Latency or Consistency.

216
00:13:32.147 --> 00:13:37.523
Without any partition, strong consistency still costs round trips to a leader or quorum.

