260
00:14:41.516 --> 00:14:42.497
L or C:

261
00:14:42.777 --> 00:14:43.396
L.

262
00:14:44.746 --> 00:14:47.050
Physical time: why clocks lie, then.

263
00:14:47.330 --> 00:14:59.063
Each machine has a quartz oscillator that drifts (roughly 10 to 100 parts per million, up to ~8.6 s a day at 100 ppm), and temperature changes the rate.

264
00:14:59.343 --> 00:15:11.674
NTP synchronizes clocks over the network to within about 1 to 10 ms on a good LAN and tens of ms across the internet, and much worse if the time server is unreachable or wrong.

265
00:15:11.954 --> 00:15:23.239
Cloud time services (AWS Time Sync, Google's) with PTP or local hardware references get to microseconds, but offsets are never zero and never guaranteed.

266
00:15:23.519 --> 00:15:24.757
Clocks also jump:

267
00:15:26.107 --> 00:15:33.000
NTP step: if the offset is large, ntpd or chrony may step the clock (possibly backwards) instead of slewing it.

268
00:15:34.280 --> 00:15:40.637
Leap seconds: some hosts step, repeat a second or smear it (Google, AWS spread it over hours).

269
00:15:40.917 --> 00:15:45.739
VM pauses and live migration: the guest's clock catches up in one jump.

270
00:15:46.019 --> 00:15:50.563
Manual changes by operators, containers inheriting a bad host clock.

271
00:15:50.843 --> 00:15:53.637
Two different clocks in every OS and in Java:

272
00:15:54.987 --> 00:15:56.353
Here is a quick table:

273
00:15:56.633 --> 00:16:00.259
Clock, and Java API, and Use for, and Never use for.

274
00:16:00.539 --> 00:16:02.203
Wall clock (time of day).

275
00:16:02.483 --> 00:16:03.678
Java API:

276
00:16:03.958 --> 00:16:08.310
Instant.now(), System.currentTimeMillis(), InstantSource or Clock.

277
00:16:08.590 --> 00:16:09.443
Use for:

278
00:16:09.723 --> 00:16:14.331
Timestamps shown to humans, created at, expiry dates stored in a DB.

279
00:16:14.611 --> 00:16:15.678
Never use for:

280
00:16:15.958 --> 00:16:19.883
Measuring durations, timeouts, ordering events across machines.

281
00:16:20.163 --> 00:16:21.358
Monotonic clock.

282
00:16:21.638 --> 00:16:22.833
Java API:

283
00:16:23.113 --> 00:16:24.563
System.nanoTime().

284
00:16:24.843 --> 00:16:25.697
Use for:

285
00:16:25.977 --> 00:16:30.265
Durations, timeouts, rate limits, lease checks inside one process.

286
00:16:30.545 --> 00:16:31.611
Never use for:

287
00:16:31.891 --> 00:16:35.603
Comparing across machines or restarts (arbitrary origin).

288
00:16:36.953 --> 00:16:48.879
L1 E: a 2 s timeout measured with the wall clock while NTP steps it back 5 s reports minus 3,800 ms elapsed and fires 5.8 s late.

289
00:16:49.159 --> 00:16:51.420
With the monotonic clock it fires on time.

290
00:16:51.700 --> 00:17:12.969
Clock skew breaks things silently: last-write-wins picks the wrong write (Ch 10 L1: a 90 s skew kept the older edit), TTL-based locks expire early or late, events sort in the wrong order (L1 A: the effect was timestamped 87 ms before its cause), and certificate validation fails (Ch 23).

291
00:17:14.319 --> 00:17:15.578
Logical clocks:

292
00:17:15.858 --> 00:17:17.778
Lamport, vector and hybrid.

293
00:17:18.058 --> 00:17:27.594
Happened-before (, then ): a, then b if a and b are on the same process in that order, or a is the send of a message and b its receipt, or transitively.

294
00:17:27.874 --> 00:17:30.946
If neither a, then b nor b, then a, they're concurrent.

295
00:17:31.226 --> 00:17:32.314
Lamport clock.

296
00:17:32.594 --> 00:17:40.935
Each process keeps a counter: increment on every event, send it with messages, on receipt set max(local, received) + 1.

297
00:17:41.215 --> 00:17:43.541
Guarantee: a, then b ⇒ L(a) L(b).

298
00:17:43.821 --> 00:17:49.111
Ties are broken by process id, which gives a total order consistent with causality.

299
00:17:50.461 --> 00:17:51.464
Limitation:

300
00:17:51.744 --> 00:17:54.005
L(a) L(b) doesn't mean a, then b.

301
00:17:54.285 --> 00:18:00.792
L1 B: an unrelated refund quote got L=2 6 even though it didn't cause the packing event.

302
00:18:01.072 --> 00:18:02.757
You can't detect concurrency.

303
00:18:03.037 --> 00:18:04.040
Vector clock.

304
00:18:04.320 --> 00:18:07.819
Each process keeps a vector of counters, one per process.

305
00:18:08.099 --> 00:18:14.712
Compare component-wise: at most in every component means happened-before, and neither at most means concurrent.

306
00:18:14.992 --> 00:18:22.757
L1 C: phone and web edits of the same cart are CONCURRENT, so the system keeps both and merges (Ch 10 OR-Set).

307
00:18:23.037 --> 00:18:28.605
Cost: vector size grows with the number of writers (use per-replica version vectors, and prune).

308
00:18:29.955 --> 00:18:33.475
Hybrid logical clock (HLC) (Kulkarni et al.

309
00:18:33.755 --> 00:18:35.099
2014).

310
00:18:35.379 --> 00:18:56.990
A timestamp (wall, logical): wall tracks the max physical time seen, and logical breaks ties and preserves causality when clocks are behind. a, then b ⇒ HLC(a) HLC(b), like Lamport. wall stays within the clock-skew bound of real physical time, so timestamps are meaningful to humans and usable for time-range queries.

311
00:18:57.270 --> 00:19:01.494
Fits in 64 bits (48-bit ms + 16-bit counter).

312
00:19:01.774 --> 00:19:09.283
L1 D: with the same 90 ms skew, PAYMENTCAPTURED 1000.0 ORDERPACKING 1000.1.

313
00:19:10.633 --> 00:19:15.625
Used by CockroachDB, YugabyteDB and MongoDB (cluster time).

314
00:19:15.905 --> 00:19:22.689
We put an HLC in every domain event's envelope (Ch 14) for ordering timelines and audit logs.

315
00:19:24.039 --> 00:19:26.749
TrueTime, commit wait and uncertainty intervals.

316
00:19:27.029 --> 00:19:29.333
Spanner doesn't pretend clocks are exact.

317
00:19:29.613 --> 00:19:41.815
TrueTime returns an interval earliest, latest guaranteed to contain real time, with ε typically a few ms thanks to GPS receivers and atomic clocks in every data centre.

318
00:19:42.095 --> 00:19:53.658
A transaction gets commit timestamp s = TT.now().latest, then waits until TT.now().earliest s before making the commit visible (commit wait, ~2ε).

319
00:19:53.938 --> 00:20:01.746
Any transaction that starts after this one finishes gets a larger timestamp, so timestamp order = real-time order.

320
00:20:02.026 --> 00:20:08.234
That's external consistency (strict serializability) from clocks, at a cost of a few ms per write.

321
00:20:09.584 --> 00:20:17.605
CockroachDB uses HLC without atomic clocks and a configured max clock offset (500 ms by default).

322
00:20:17.885 --> 00:20:26.995
A read that sees a value with a timestamp inside its uncertainty window can't tell whether it's before or after, so it restarts at a higher timestamp.

323
00:20:27.275 --> 00:20:32.992
Nodes whose clock offset exceeds the bound shut themselves down, because correctness depends on it.

324
00:20:33.272 --> 00:20:40.141
The lesson for everyone else: if correctness depends on clocks, the bound must be explicit, monitored and enforced.

325
00:20:41.491 --> 00:20:42.899
The consensus problem.

326
00:20:43.179 --> 00:20:49.729
Several nodes must agree on a value (or on a sequence of values, which is a replicated log) such that:

327
00:20:50.009 --> 00:20:52.590
Agreement: no two nodes decide differently.

328
00:20:52.870 --> 00:20:56.177
Validity: the decided value was proposed by someone.

329
00:20:56.457 --> 00:20:59.998
Termination: every non-failed node eventually decides.

330
00:21:01.348 --> 00:21:13.999
FLP impossibility (Fischer, Lynch, Paterson, 1985): in a fully asynchronous system where even one process may crash, no deterministic algorithm guarantees all three.

331
00:21:14.279 --> 00:21:27.441
Practical algorithms (Paxos, Raft) keep safety always (never two decisions) and get liveness only when the network behaves well enough for long enough (partial synchrony), using timeouts and randomization.

332
00:21:27.721 --> 00:21:34.889
Majorities: with 2f + 1 nodes, the system tolerates f failures, because any two majorities intersect.

333
00:21:36.239 --> 00:21:37.605
