368
00:22:24.930 --> 00:22:25.677
Note:

369
00:22:25.957 --> 00:22:27.727
Rarely worth the write latency.

370
00:22:29.077 --> 00:22:32.363
What consensus gives you: a replicated state machine.

371
00:22:32.643 --> 00:22:38.168
If every node applies the same log of commands in the same order, all nodes have the same state.

372
00:22:38.448 --> 00:22:49.648
That's the foundation of etcd, ZooKeeper (Zab), Consul, Kafka KRaft, CockroachDB or TiKV ranges (Raft per range), Spanner (Paxos per split) and Chubby.

373
00:22:49.928 --> 00:22:56.221
Consensus about linearizable CAS about atomic broadcast about leader election with a fence.

374
00:22:56.501 --> 00:22:57.995
They're equivalent problems.

375
00:22:58.275 --> 00:23:00.387
If you have one, you can build the others.

376
00:23:01.737 --> 00:23:02.782
Raft in depth.

377
00:23:03.062 --> 00:23:09.355
Raft (Ongaro and Ousterhout, 2014) splits consensus into understandable parts.

378
00:23:09.635 --> 00:23:10.425
Terms.

379
00:23:10.705 --> 00:23:14.673
Time is divided into numbered terms, each with at most one leader.

380
00:23:14.953 --> 00:23:19.433
A node that sees a higher term immediately adopts it and becomes a follower.

381
00:23:19.713 --> 00:23:22.358
Terms are the fencing tokens inside Raft.

382
00:23:22.638 --> 00:23:23.747
Leader election.

383
00:23:24.027 --> 00:23:40.411
A follower that hears nothing from a leader for a randomized election timeout (for example, 150 to 300 ms) becomes a candidate, increments its term, votes for itself and sends RequestVote(term, lastLogIndex, lastLogTerm).

384
00:23:41.761 --> 00:23:53.623
A node grants at most one vote per term, and only if the candidate's log is at least as up-to-date as its own: a higher last term, or the same last term and an index at least as long.

385
00:23:53.903 --> 00:23:58.020
With votes from a majority, the candidate becomes leader and sends heartbeats.

386
00:23:58.300 --> 00:24:00.604
Randomized timeouts make split votes rare.

387
00:24:00.884 --> 00:24:02.164
Log replication.

388
00:24:02.444 --> 00:24:10.743
The leader appends a client command to its log and sends AppendEntries(term, prevLogIndex, prevLogTerm, entries, leaderCommit).

389
00:24:12.093 --> 00:24:17.639
A follower accepts only if its log has an entry at prevLogIndex with prevLogTerm.

390
00:24:17.919 --> 00:24:20.970
Otherwise it rejects, and the leader backs up and retries.

391
00:24:21.250 --> 00:24:24.663
Conflicting follower entries are overwritten by the leader's.

392
00:24:24.943 --> 00:24:34.458
That consistency check gives the Log Matching property: if two logs have an entry with the same index and term, the logs are identical up to that entry.

393
00:24:35.808 --> 00:24:36.704
Commit rule.

394
00:24:36.984 --> 00:24:42.957
An entry is committed when the leader has replicated it to a majority and it's from the leader's current term.

395
00:24:43.237 --> 00:24:48.677
Earlier-term entries are committed only indirectly, when a current-term entry after them commits.

396
00:24:48.957 --> 00:24:52.307
That's why a new leader immediately appends a no-op entry.

397
00:24:52.587 --> 00:25:03.701
L3 runs the paper's Figure 8 scenario: with the rule broken (committing an old-term entry just because it's on a majority), a later leader overwrites a committed entry.

398
00:25:03.981 --> 00:25:07.245
With the correct rule, the entry was never declared committed.

399
00:25:07.525 --> 00:25:10.853
Safety properties (all checked continuously in L3):

400
00:25:12.203 --> 00:25:13.569
Here is a quick table:

401
00:25:13.849 --> 00:25:16.153
Property, and Meaning, and L3 check.

402
00:25:16.433 --> 00:25:17.627
Election Safety.

403
00:25:17.907 --> 00:25:18.697
Meaning:

404
00:25:18.977 --> 00:25:20.555
At most one leader per term.

405
00:25:20.835 --> 00:25:22.990
L3 check: leaderOfTerm map.

406
00:25:23.270 --> 00:25:24.294
Log Matching.

407
00:25:24.574 --> 00:25:25.363
Meaning:

408
00:25:25.643 --> 00:25:27.798
Same index + term ⇒ same prefix.

409
00:25:28.078 --> 00:25:29.123
L3 check:

410
00:25:29.403 --> 00:25:31.067
Implied by the append check.

411
00:25:31.347 --> 00:25:32.627
Leader Completeness.

412
00:25:32.907 --> 00:25:33.697
Meaning:

413
00:25:33.977 --> 00:25:36.878
A committed entry is in every later leader's log.

414
00:25:37.158 --> 00:25:38.203
L3 check:

415
00:25:38.483 --> 00:25:39.977
Checked at every election.

416
00:25:40.257 --> 00:25:41.622
State Machine Safety.

417
00:25:41.902 --> 00:25:42.691
Meaning:

418
00:25:42.971 --> 00:25:46.278
No two nodes apply different commands at the same index.

419
00:25:46.558 --> 00:25:47.603
L3 check:

420
00:25:47.883 --> 00:25:49.227
Checked at every apply.

421
00:25:50.577 --> 00:26:00.028
L3 ran 300 randomized runs per variant (5 nodes, 20 s each, 5 % message loss, partitions, crash or restarts).

422
00:26:00.308 --> 00:26:10.719
The correct Raft had 0 violations over 23,845 elections and 416,335 committed entries.

423
00:26:10.999 --> 00:26:16.780
Removing the up-to-date vote check broke safety in 294 or 300 runs.

424
00:26:17.060 --> 00:26:24.185
The broken commit rule showed 0 or 300 in random runs and failed only in the targeted Figure 8 script.

425
00:26:24.465 --> 00:26:33.681
That's a lesson in itself: random testing misses rare interleavings, so write the known-dangerous scenarios as explicit tests (Jepsen does both).

426
00:26:33.961 --> 00:26:35.007
Beyond the core:

427
00:26:36.357 --> 00:26:45.893
Membership changes: add or remove one server at a time, or use joint consensus, so two disjoint majorities can never exist during a change.

428
00:26:46.173 --> 00:26:51.997
Log compaction: snapshots of the state machine plus InstallSnapshot for lagging followers.

429
00:26:52.277 --> 00:27:00.746
PreVote and check-quorum: a node first asks would you vote for me? without bumping terms, so a flapping node can't disrupt a healthy leader.

430
00:27:01.026 --> 00:27:03.650
A leader that can't reach a majority steps down.

431
00:27:04.000 --> 00:27:15.475
Client interaction: commands carry a client id and sequence number, so a retried command after a leader change is applied once (idempotency, Ch 20).

432
00:27:16.825 --> 00:27:18.531
Paxos, Zab and friends.

433
00:27:18.811 --> 00:27:20.177
