Record the commit index, confirm leadership with a heartbeat round acked by a majority, wait until applied ≥ that index, then answer.

484
00:29:46.331 --> 00:29:48.102
Stale reads in the simulation:

485
00:29:48.382 --> 00:29:52.222
0 of 321,661.

486
00:29:52.502 --> 00:29:53.227
Cost:

487
00:29:53.507 --> 00:29:55.470
One round trip per read (batched).

488
00:29:55.750 --> 00:29:56.689
Log read.

489
00:29:56.969 --> 00:29:57.630
How:

490
00:29:57.910 --> 00:29:59.830
Put the read through the log like a write.

491
00:30:00.110 --> 00:30:01.881
Stale reads in the simulation:

492
00:30:02.161 --> 00:30:02.907
0.

493
00:30:03.187 --> 00:30:03.913
Cost:

494
00:30:04.193 --> 00:30:05.601
A full write per read.

495
00:30:06.951 --> 00:30:07.911
Lease read.

496
00:30:08.191 --> 00:30:08.852
How:

497
00:30:09.132 --> 00:30:18.199
After a majority acks a heartbeat, the leader answers locally until sentAt + lease (lease minimum election timeout), measured on its own clock.

498
00:30:18.479 --> 00:30:20.249
Stale reads in the simulation:

499
00:30:20.529 --> 00:30:27.612
0 with accurate clocks and 10 % drift; 298 when the leader's clock ran 50 % slow.

500
00:30:27.892 --> 00:30:28.617
Cost:

501
00:30:28.897 --> 00:30:31.969
No round trip, but depends on bounded clock drift.

502
00:30:32.249 --> 00:30:33.337
Follower read.

503
00:30:33.617 --> 00:30:34.279
How:

504
00:30:34.559 --> 00:30:38.228
A follower asks the leader for the ReadIndex, then serves locally.

505
00:30:38.508 --> 00:30:40.279
Stale reads in the simulation:

506
00:30:40.559 --> 00:30:41.305
0.

507
00:30:41.585 --> 00:30:42.311
Cost:

508
00:30:42.591 --> 00:30:43.615
Spreads load.

509
00:30:44.965 --> 00:30:54.970
Bug found while building ReadIndex: the first version answered a pending read as soon as the node had applied up to the read index, before the majority confirmation came back.

510
00:30:55.250 --> 00:31:02.205
It produced 355 stale reads, and the fix was to answer only rounds that a majority had confirmed.

511
00:31:02.485 --> 00:31:05.557
That's exactly the kind of bug that passes code review.

512
00:31:05.837 --> 00:31:07.287
The safety checks caught it.

513
00:31:08.637 --> 00:31:11.944
In etcd, Get is linearizable by default (ReadIndex).

514
00:31:12.224 --> 00:31:20.757
With WithSerializable() (etcdctl get --consistency=s) a member answers from local state, which is fast but possibly stale.

515
00:31:21.037 --> 00:31:30.403
Lab 2 shows a member cut off from its peers refusing the linearizable read and answering the serializable one with a value that was already overwritten.

516
00:31:30.683 --> 00:31:36.933
ZooKeeper reads are local (sequential consistency), and sync() before a read makes it linearizable.

517
00:31:38.283 --> 00:31:49.995
Lease reads also need leader stickiness: a follower that heard from the leader recently must refuse to vote for anyone else, otherwise a new leader could be elected while the old lease is still valid.

518
00:31:50.275 --> 00:31:56.291
The simulation's fault model (symmetric partitions, message loss, crashes) didn't produce that case:

519
00:31:56.571 --> 00:31:58.662
0 stale reads without stickiness.

520
00:31:58.942 --> 00:32:07.795
Asymmetric partitions (A hears B, B doesn't hear A) do, which is why etcd and TiKV pair lease reads with check-quorum or PreVote.

