Service mesh or discovery.

559
00:33:31.195 --> 00:33:41.883
Recipes built on these: leader election, distributed locks (with fencing), configuration with atomic updates and watches, service discovery, barriers, and membership.

560
00:33:42.163 --> 00:33:55.048
Limits: these are small, strongly consistent metadata stores. etcd recommends keeping the database to a few GB (default quota 2 GB) and requests small (1.5 MiB max).

561
00:33:55.328 --> 00:34:01.771
Write throughput is thousands to tens of thousands per second, bounded by fsync and majority round trips.

562
00:34:02.051 --> 00:34:04.035
Don't put orders or carts in etcd.

563
00:34:05.385 --> 00:34:07.155
Leases and fencing tokens.

564
00:34:07.435 --> 00:34:14.923
A lease is a lock with an expiry: whoever holds it may act until it expires, so a crashed holder doesn't block others forever.

565
00:34:15.203 --> 00:34:17.422
But leases rest on timing assumptions:

566
00:34:17.702 --> 00:34:24.614
The holder can pause (GC, VM migration, swap, a blocked syscall) past the expiry without noticing.

567
00:34:24.894 --> 00:34:28.457
After waking it checks nothing, or checks a stale local timer.

568
00:34:28.737 --> 00:34:32.129
Its clock may run slow, so it thinks the lease has time left.

569
00:34:32.409 --> 00:34:37.742
Network delay means a write it sent while it held the lease can arrive after the lease expired.

570
00:34:39.092 --> 00:34:43.935
So a lease guarantees at most one holder at any instant according to the lease store.

571
00:34:44.215 --> 00:34:46.881
It doesn't guarantee at most one process acting.

572
00:34:47.161 --> 00:34:50.724
The fix (Kleppmann's fencing tokens, and Chubby's sequencers):

573
00:34:51.004 --> 00:34:59.921
The lease store hands out a monotonically increasing token with every grant: an epoch, a Raft term, an etcd revision, a ZooKeeper zxid.

574
00:35:00.201 --> 00:35:03.977
The holder sends the token with every write to the protected resource.

575
00:35:04.257 --> 00:35:12.812
The resource rejects any write with a token lower than the highest it has seen: update... set fence =:t where... and fence =:t.

576
00:35:14.162 --> 00:35:26.983
The lab shows it on real PostgreSQL: without the check, a pod that paused for 6 s overwrote its successor's payout (41250.00 writtenby pod-A).

577
00:35:27.263 --> 00:35:37.503
With the check, the stale write updated 0 rows and the correct payout stayed (39980.00 writtenby pod-B fence 2).

578
00:35:37.783 --> 00:35:39.511
The resource must participate.

579
00:35:39.791 --> 00:35:50.991
If the resource can't check tokens (an external email API, a bank file drop), make the operation idempotent with a key derived from the business action (Ch 20).

580
00:35:51.271 --> 00:35:57.394
Or have the leader write its intent to a fenced table, and let a single dispatcher perform the side effect.

581
00:35:58.744 --> 00:36:06.637
Redlock (Redis locks across 5 independent masters) doesn't provide fencing tokens and relies on timing assumptions.

582
00:36:06.917 --> 00:36:15.429
It's fine for efficiency locks (avoid duplicate work) and not for correctness locks (Kleppmann versus Sanfilippo, 2016).

583
00:36:15.709 --> 00:36:25.501
The §1.1 incident used a single Redis lock, which is worse again: a failover to an async replica can lose the lock key (Ch 10).

584
00:36:26.851 --> 00:36:28.451
When not to use consensus.

585
00:36:28.731 --> 00:36:35.110
Consensus costs a majority round trip per write (cross-AZ: ~1 to 3 ms, cross-region:

586
00:36:35.390 --> 00:36:42.537
50 to 200 ms), limits throughput, needs 3 plus nodes, and becomes unavailable without a majority.

587
00:36:42.817 --> 00:36:43.819
Avoid it when:

588
00:36:44.099 --> 00:36:51.651
One authority already exists: a single PostgreSQL primary per shard is already linearizable for its data.

589
00:36:51.931 --> 00:36:53.574
Use its row locks, SELECT...

590
00:36:53.854 --> 00:36:56.862
FOR UPDATE, unique constraints or advisory locks.

591
00:36:57.142 --> 00:37:04.715
Conflicts can be merged: carts, likes, counters, presence, then CRDTs or LWW with care (Ch 10).

592
00:37:06.065 --> 00:37:12.700
Duplicates are harmless or preventable: idempotent consumers are simpler than exactly-one executors.

593
00:37:12.980 --> 00:37:23.924
Coordination can be avoided by design: partition the work so each partition has one owner (Kafka consumer groups, Ch 14), or make the operation commutative.

594
00:37:24.204 --> 00:37:35.468
The principle behind this is coordination avoidance (Bailis et al.; the CALM theorem): coordinate only for invariants that truly need it, such as money, uniqueness and leadership.

