01:19:24.769 --> 01:19:25.515
3.

1160
01:19:25.795 --> 01:19:26.585
Effect:

1161
01:19:26.865 --> 01:19:33.691
5 tolerates 2 failures; more members = slower writes. etcd heartbeat-interval or election-timeout.

1162
01:19:33.971 --> 01:19:34.889
Default here:

1163
01:19:35.169 --> 01:19:37.601
100 ms or 1000 ms.

1164
01:19:37.881 --> 01:19:38.670
Effect:

1165
01:19:38.950 --> 01:19:43.643
Lower = faster failover, more false elections on slow disks or networks.

1166
01:19:43.923 --> 01:19:45.779
Lease TTL or keep-alive.

1167
01:19:46.059 --> 01:19:46.977
Default here:

1168
01:19:47.257 --> 01:19:48.579
10 s or 3 s.

1169
01:19:48.859 --> 01:19:49.649
Effect:

1170
01:19:49.929 --> 01:19:53.833
Lower = faster failover, more churn on GC pauses.

1171
01:19:54.113 --> 01:19:56.225
Leader's local validity margin.

1172
01:19:56.505 --> 01:19:57.422
Default here:

1173
01:19:57.702 --> 01:19:59.323
TTL minus 3 s.

1174
01:19:59.603 --> 01:20:00.393
Effect:

1175
01:20:00.673 --> 01:20:03.766
Bigger margin = stops earlier on uncertainty.

1176
01:20:04.046 --> 01:20:05.006
Read mode.

1177
01:20:05.286 --> 01:20:06.203
Default here:

1178
01:20:06.483 --> 01:20:09.961
Linearizable for CAS paths; watch cache for routing.

1179
01:20:10.241 --> 01:20:11.030
Effect:

1180
01:20:11.310 --> 01:20:12.953
Round trip versus staleness.

1181
01:20:14.303 --> 01:20:15.135
Batching.

1182
01:20:15.415 --> 01:20:18.572
Default here: etcd batches proposals automatically.

1183
01:20:18.852 --> 01:20:19.641
Effect:

1184
01:20:19.921 --> 01:20:21.244
Throughput under load.

1185
01:20:21.524 --> 01:20:23.359
HLC max forward drift.

1186
01:20:23.639 --> 01:20:24.556
Default here:

1187
01:20:24.836 --> 01:20:26.180
500 ms.

1188
01:20:26.460 --> 01:20:27.249
Effect:

1189
01:20:27.529 --> 01:20:31.775
Rejects faulty upstream clocks versus false alarms. chrony makestep.

1190
01:20:32.055 --> 01:20:32.972
Default here:

1191
01:20:33.252 --> 01:20:34.340
Only at boot.

1192
01:20:34.620 --> 01:20:35.409
Effect:

1193
01:20:35.689 --> 01:20:38.271
No steps during operation (slew only).

1194
01:20:39.621 --> 01:20:40.346
Cost.

1195
01:20:40.626 --> 01:20:47.986
A 3-member etcd cluster on small dedicated VMs is cheap compared to one incident like §1.1.

1196
01:20:48.266 --> 01:20:54.581
The real costs are operations (upgrades, defrag, backup or restore drills) and on-call knowledge.

1197
01:20:54.861 --> 01:21:04.994
Kubernetes already runs etcd, but don't put application coordination into the cluster's own etcd unless you accept that an application bug can hurt the control plane.

1198
01:21:05.274 --> 01:21:10.330
Use Lease objects through the API server (rate-limited), or run a separate etcd.

1199
01:21:10.610 --> 01:21:15.922
Linearizable reads cost a round trip each, and at high QPS that's real capacity.

1200
01:21:16.202 --> 01:21:19.167
Use watch-fed caches plus fences for routing.

1201
01:21:20.517 --> 01:21:24.741
Consensus across regions costs 50 to 200 ms per write.

1202
01:21:25.021 --> 01:21:35.176
Keep quorums in-region and replicate asynchronously across regions unless RPO 0 across regions is a business requirement (Ch 27).

1203
01:21:36.526 --> 01:21:37.465
Part 7.

1204
01:21:37.745 --> 01:21:39.451
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

1205
01:21:40.701 --> 01:21:41.341
1.

1206
01:21:41.621 --> 01:21:44.480
A lock with a TTL, and no fencing token.

1207
01:21:44.760 --> 01:21:45.571
Symptom:

1208
01:21:45.851 --> 01:21:51.269
A paused leader wakes up and writes over its successor (§1.1, lab run 1).

1209
01:21:52.619 --> 01:22:01.643
Here is the SQL code. before: the lock is checked in the app, the write trusts it. after: the resource enforces the newest leader's token.

1210
01:22:04.493 --> 01:22:05.197
2.

1211
01:22:05.477 --> 01:22:07.739
Measuring durations with the wall clock.

1212
01:22:09.089 --> 01:22:11.137
Here is the Java code. before:

1213
01:22:11.417 --> 01:22:23.641
NTP steps, VM resumes and manual changes make this negative or huge (L1 E: minus 3,800 ms). after: monotonic, immune to clock changes.

1214
01:22:26.491 --> 01:22:27.237
3.

1215
01:22:27.517 --> 01:22:31.229
Ordering events from different machines by wall-clock timestamps.

1216
01:22:31.509 --> 01:22:32.320
Symptom:

1217
01:22:32.600 --> 01:22:35.203
Packing before Paid in the timeline (L1 A).

1218
01:22:35.483 --> 01:22:36.208
Fix:

1219
01:22:36.488 --> 01:22:41.437
HLC (or Lamport + tie-break) in the envelope, sorted by HLC.

1220
01:22:41.717 --> 01:22:42.421
4.

1221
01:22:42.701 --> 01:22:45.027
Assuming quorum reads are linearizable.

1222
01:22:45.307 --> 01:22:46.117
Symptom:

1223
01:22:46.397 --> 01:22:50.579
A reader sees a new value and a later reader sees the old one (L2:

1224
01:22:50.859 --> 01:22:53.461
0.06 % of histories).

1225
01:22:53.741 --> 01:22:54.467
Fix:

1226
01:22:54.747 --> 01:23:00.315
Read repair or write-back before returning (ABD), or read through the leader with ReadIndex.

1227
01:23:01.665 --> 01:23:02.433
5.

1228
01:23:02.713 --> 01:23:06.638
Reading from the leader's local state and calling it strongly consistent.

1229
01:23:06.918 --> 01:23:07.729
Symptom:

1230
01:23:08.009 --> 01:23:12.297
A deposed leader in a minority partition keeps answering (L3:

1231
01:23:12.577 --> 01:23:15.734
66,224 stale reads).

1232
01:23:16.014 --> 01:23:16.739
Fix:

1233
01:23:17.019 --> 01:23:20.475
ReadIndex, or leases with bounded drift and check-quorum.

1234
01:23:20.755 --> 01:23:23.870
In etcd, don't use serializable reads for decisions.

1235
01:23:24.150 --> 01:23:24.897
6.

1236
01:23:25.177 --> 01:23:28.249
Using a 2-node or 4-node consensus cluster.

1237
01:23:28.529 --> 01:23:29.339
Symptom:

1238
01:23:29.619 --> 01:23:31.923
2 nodes: one failure stops everything.

1239
01:23:32.203 --> 01:23:36.321
4 nodes: tolerates 1, the same as 3, with more to replicate.

1240
01:23:36.601 --> 01:23:37.326
Fix:

1241
01:23:37.606 --> 01:23:40.443
3 or 5 members, in different failure domains.

1242
01:23:41.793 --> 01:23:42.540
7.

1243
01:23:42.820 --> 01:23:45.807
Putting application data in etcd or ZooKeeper.

1244
01:23:46.087 --> 01:23:46.897
Symptom:

1245
01:23:47.177 --> 01:23:55.540
DB size near quota, slow fsyncs, elections flapping, and Kubernetes or Patroni affected by an application's write load.

1246
01:23:55.820 --> 01:23:56.545
Fix:

1247
01:23:56.825 --> 01:23:58.063
Metadata only.

1248
01:23:58.343 --> 01:24:00.071
Data goes in databases.

1249
01:24:00.351 --> 01:24:01.033
8.

1250
01:24:01.313 --> 01:24:06.817
Treating LWW with wall clocks as conflict resolution for important data.

1251
01:24:07.097 --> 01:24:07.908
Symptom:

1252
01:24:08.188 --> 01:24:11.388
Silent loss of the newer update (Ch 10 L1).

1253
01:24:11.668 --> 01:24:12.393
Fix:

1254
01:24:12.673 --> 01:24:17.964
Versions or CAS, vector clocks with merges, or a single authoritative writer.

1255
01:24:19.314 --> 01:24:20.061
9.

1256
01:24:20.341 --> 01:24:22.261
Believing CAP means pick two.

1257
01:24:22.541 --> 01:24:23.351
Symptom:

1258
01:24:23.631 --> 01:24:26.810
Design docs claiming CA for a distributed store.

1259
01:24:27.090 --> 01:24:27.815
Fix:

1260
01:24:28.095 --> 01:24:36.479
Partitions happen: state what each operation does during a partition (refuse or answer stale), and what it costs without one (PACELC).

1261
01:24:36.759 --> 01:24:37.463
10.

1262
01:24:37.743 --> 01:24:39.557
Trusting that tests found no bug.

1263
01:24:39.837 --> 01:24:40.647
Symptom:

1264
01:24:40.927 --> 01:24:51.658
The broken Raft commit rule passed 300 random runs (L3), and the first ReadIndex implementation served 355 stale reads before the fix.

1265
01:24:51.938 --> 01:24:52.663
Fix:

1266
01:24:52.943 --> 01:25:02.821
Invariant checks everywhere, targeted scenario tests for known-dangerous interleavings, and Jepsen-style fault injection for anything coordination-related.

1267
01:25:04.171 --> 01:25:05.045
Part 8.

1268
01:25:05.325 --> 01:25:06.669
Interview Q and A.

1269
01:25:07.919 --> 01:25:08.751
Beginner.

1270
01:25:09.031 --> 01:25:10.055
Question 1.

1271
01:25:10.335 --> 01:25:13.642
What's the difference between strong and eventual consistency?

1272
01:25:13.922 --> 01:25:15.287
Thirty-second answer:

1273
01:25:15.567 --> 01:25:24.314
Strong (linearizable) consistency makes a replicated system behave like one copy: once a write completes, every later read sees it.

1274
01:25:24.594 --> 01:25:32.722
Eventual consistency only promises that replicas converge if writes stop, so reads in between may be stale or out of order.

1275
01:25:34.072 --> 01:25:35.075
Question 2.

1276
01:25:35.355 --> 01:25:38.960
Why can't we trust timestamps from different servers to order events?

1277
01:25:39.240 --> 01:25:40.605
Thirty-second answer:

1278
01:25:40.885 --> 01:25:49.120
Every machine's clock drifts and is corrected by NTP only to within milliseconds, sometimes much worse, and clocks can jump.

1279
01:25:49.400 --> 01:25:54.648
Two events a few ms apart on different machines can get timestamps in the wrong order.

1280
01:25:54.928 --> 01:26:00.133
L1: a 90 ms skew timestamped the effect 87 ms before its cause.

1281
01:26:01.483 --> 01:26:02.529
Question 3.

1282
01:26:02.809 --> 01:26:04.835
What problem does consensus solve?

1283
01:26:05.115 --> 01:26:06.481
Thirty-second answer:

1284
01:26:06.761 --> 01:26:16.702
Getting several machines to agree on a value or a sequence of values (a log), even when some crash or the network misbehaves, so that no two decide differently.

1285
01:26:16.982 --> 01:26:24.939
It's used for leader election, configuration, locks, and replicated state machines like etcd, ZooKeeper and Kafka's KRaft.

1286
01:26:26.289 --> 01:26:27.356
Question 4.

1287
01:26:27.636 --> 01:26:31.647
What is CAP in one sentence, and what's the common misunderstanding?

1288
01:26:31.927 --> 01:26:33.292
Thirty-second answer:

1289
01:26:33.572 --> 01:26:43.791
When the network partitions, a replicated store must choose between answering every request (availability) and always returning the latest value (linearizability).

1290
01:26:44.071 --> 01:26:50.705
The misunderstanding is pick 2 of 3: partitions aren't optional, so the real choice is what to do during one.

1291
01:26:52.055 --> 01:26:53.058
Intermediate.

1292
01:26:53.338 --> 01:26:54.447
Question 5.

1293
01:26:54.727 --> 01:26:57.415
Linearizability versus serializability?

1294
01:26:57.695 --> 01:26:59.061
Thirty-second answer:

1295
01:26:59.341 --> 01:27:10.477
Serializability is a transaction isolation guarantee: concurrent transactions behave as if run one at a time in some order, not necessarily real-time order.

1296
01:27:10.757 --> 01:27:20.527
Linearizability is a recency guarantee on single objects: each operation appears to happen instantly between its start and end, respecting real time.

1297
01:27:20.807 --> 01:27:23.367
Both together is strict serializability.

1298
01:27:24.717 --> 01:27:25.827
Question 6.

1299
01:27:26.107 --> 01:27:28.283
Lamport clocks versus vector clocks?

1300
01:27:28.563 --> 01:27:29.928
Thirty-second answer:

1301
01:27:30.208 --> 01:27:39.296
A Lamport clock is a single counter that guarantees cause effect, but a smaller timestamp doesn't prove causality, so it can't detect concurrency.

1302
01:27:39.576 --> 01:27:48.088
A vector clock keeps a counter per process and can tell happened-before from concurrent, at the cost of size that grows with the number of processes.

1303
01:27:49.438 --> 01:27:50.526
Question 7.

1304
01:27:50.806 --> 01:27:53.899
What's a hybrid logical clock and why would you use one?

1305
01:27:54.179 --> 01:27:55.545
Thirty-second answer:

1306
01:27:55.825 --> 01:28:03.867
A timestamp combining physical time with a logical counter: wall is the max physical time seen, logical breaks ties.

1307
01:28:04.147 --> 01:28:14.345
It preserves causality like a Lamport clock but stays close to real time, so it's usable for time-range queries and human debugging, and fits in 64 bits.

1308
01:28:15.695 --> 01:28:16.719
Question 8.

1309
01:28:16.999 --> 01:28:19.260
Why must timeouts use a monotonic clock?

1310
01:28:19.540 --> 01:28:20.905
Thirty-second answer:

1311
01:28:21.185 --> 01:28:29.804
Wall clocks can be stepped by NTP, leap handling, VM resume or operators, so now minus start can go negative or jump.

1312
01:28:30.084 --> 01:28:33.177
A monotonic clock only moves forward at a steady rate.

1313
01:28:33.457 --> 01:28:45.340
L1 E: with a minus 5 s NTP step, a 2 s timeout measured on the wall clock computed minus 3,800 ms elapsed and fired 5.8 s late.

1314
01:28:46.690 --> 01:28:47.757
Question 9.

1315
01:28:48.037 --> 01:28:51.365
Are quorum reads (R + W N) linearizable?

1316
01:28:51.645 --> 01:28:53.010
Thirty-second answer:

1317
01:28:53.290 --> 01:28:54.506
Not by themselves.

1318
01:28:54.786 --> 01:29:02.978
They guarantee you see the latest completed write, but during an in-flight write one reader can see the new value and a later reader the old one.

1319
01:29:03.258 --> 01:29:06.906
L2 found this in 0.06 % of histories.

1320
01:29:07.186 --> 01:29:11.581
Adding a write-back phase before returning (ABD) makes them linearizable:

1321
01:29:11.861 --> 01:29:13.887
0 in 20,000 histories.

1322
01:29:15.237 --> 01:29:16.005
Senior.

1323
01:29:16.285 --> 01:29:17.331
Question 10.

1324
01:29:17.611 --> 01:29:20.960
Walk me through Raft leader election and log replication.

1325
01:29:21.240 --> 01:29:22.605
Thirty-second answer:

1326
01:29:22.885 --> 01:29:28.496
Followers that time out (randomized) become candidates, increment the term and ask for votes.

1327
01:29:28.776 --> 01:29:34.131
A node grants one vote per term, only to candidates whose log is at least as up-to-date.

1328
01:29:34.411 --> 01:29:35.968
A majority makes a leader.

1329
01:29:36.248 --> 01:29:41.773
The leader appends commands and replicates them with a consistency check on the previous entry.

1330
01:29:42.053 --> 01:29:48.219
An entry from the current term is committed once it's on a majority, and committed entries are applied in order.

1331
01:29:49.569 --> 01:29:50.699
Question 11.

1332
01:29:50.979 --> 01:29:56.142
Why can't a Raft leader commit an entry from an earlier term just because a majority has it?

1333
01:29:56.422 --> 01:29:57.787
Thirty-second answer:

1334
01:29:58.067 --> 01:30:04.489
Because a node with a higher last term, but missing that entry, can still win a later election and overwrite it.

1335
01:30:04.769 --> 01:30:06.667
That's the Raft paper's Figure 8.

1336
01:30:06.947 --> 01:30:10.531
A leader counts replicas only for entries of its current term.

1337
01:30:10.811 --> 01:30:13.414
Earlier entries become committed indirectly.

1338
01:30:14.764 --> 01:30:15.852
Question 12.

1339
01:30:16.132 --> 01:30:18.351
How does etcd serve a linearizable read?

1340
01:30:18.631 --> 01:30:19.996
Thirty-second answer:

1341
01:30:20.276 --> 01:30:21.364
With ReadIndex.

1342
01:30:21.644 --> 01:30:32.929
The leader records its commit index, confirms it's still leader with a heartbeat round acknowledged by a majority, waits until its state machine has applied up to that index, then answers.

1343
01:30:33.209 --> 01:30:38.863
A serializable read answers from any member's local state, which is faster but may be stale.

1344
01:30:40.213 --> 01:30:41.493
Question 13.

1345
01:30:41.773 --> 01:30:44.653
How would you implement leader election for a batch job?

1346
01:30:44.933 --> 01:30:46.298
Thirty-second answer:

1347
01:30:46.578 --> 01:30:54.578
Campaign on an etcd election (or a ZooKeeper ephemeral sequential node, or a Kubernetes Lease) with a lease and keep-alives.

1348
01:30:54.858 --> 01:30:57.823
Use the leader key's revision as a fencing token.

1349
01:30:58.103 --> 01:31:00.834
Before each unit of work, check local leadership.

1350
01:31:01.114 --> 01:31:05.509
Pass the token with every write, and let the resource reject stale tokens.

1351
01:31:05.789 --> 01:31:07.943
Make external side effects idempotent.

1352
01:31:09.293 --> 01:31:10.488
Question 14.

1353
01:31:10.768 --> 01:31:15.419
What are fencing tokens, and why doesn't a lock service alone prevent split brain?

1354
01:31:15.699 --> 01:31:17.064
Thirty-second answer:

1355
01:31:17.344 --> 01:31:27.115
A monotonically increasing number issued with each lock grant, sent with every write and checked by the resource, which rejects anything older than the newest it has seen.

1356
01:31:27.395 --> 01:31:35.885
A lock service decides who holds the lock now, but a paused or delayed former holder doesn't know it lost the lock and can still send writes.

1357
01:31:36.165 --> 01:31:38.043
Only the resource can reject them.

1358
01:31:39.393 --> 01:31:40.289
Architect.

1359
01:31:40.569 --> 01:31:41.763
Question 15.

1360
01:31:42.043 --> 01:31:47.825
How does Spanner achieve external consistency, and what do databases without atomic clocks do?

1361
01:31:48.105 --> 01:31:49.470
Thirty-second answer:

1362
01:31:49.750 --> 01:31:53.889
TrueTime returns an interval guaranteed to contain real time.

1363
01:31:54.169 --> 01:32:04.665
A transaction picks commit timestamp TT.now().latest and waits until TT.now().earliest passes it (commit wait, about 2ε) before becoming visible.

1364
01:32:04.945 --> 01:32:08.166
So later transactions always get larger timestamps.

1365
01:32:08.446 --> 01:32:20.777
CockroachDB uses HLC with a max clock offset: reads that find values inside their uncertainty window restart at a higher timestamp, and nodes beyond the offset bound shut down.

1366
01:32:22.127 --> 01:32:23.321
Question 16.

1367
01:32:23.601 --> 01:32:26.716
When would you use lease-based reads instead of ReadIndex?

1368
01:32:26.996 --> 01:32:28.361
Thirty-second answer:

1369
01:32:28.641 --> 01:32:37.345
When read latency matters more than a small risk tied to clock behaviour, and you can bound clock drift and enable check-quorum or leader stickiness.

1370
01:32:37.625 --> 01:32:43.855
The leader serves locally until its lease (less than the election timeout, adjusted for drift) expires.

1371
01:32:44.135 --> 01:32:44.988
L3:

1372
01:32:45.268 --> 01:32:52.841
0 stale reads with accurate or 10 % slow clocks, and 298 with a 50 % slow leader clock.

1373
01:32:54.191 --> 01:32:55.535
Question 17.

1374
01:32:55.815 --> 01:32:58.994
How do you test a consensus or coordination system?

1375
01:32:59.274 --> 01:33:00.639
Thirty-second answer:

1376
01:33:00.919 --> 01:33:19.543
Deterministic simulation with fault injection (delays, loss, partitions, crashes, clock skew), invariant checks on every step, targeted scenario tests for known dangerous interleavings, linearizability checking of client histories, and Jepsen-style tests against the real system.

1377
01:33:20.893 --> 01:33:22.067
Question 18.

1378
01:33:22.347 --> 01:33:25.589
Design the coordination layer for the grocery platform.

1379
01:33:25.869 --> 01:33:27.235
Thirty-second answer:

1380
01:33:27.515 --> 01:33:40.827
A 3-member etcd across AZs for metadata only: leader elections with leases and fencing tokens, the shard map with CAS updates and gap-free watches, feature flags, and Patroni's DCS.

1381
01:33:41.107 --> 01:33:42.941
Resources enforce tokens.

1382
01:33:43.221 --> 01:33:44.779
Events carry HLCs.

1383
01:33:45.059 --> 01:33:47.277
Hosts run chrony with offset alerting.

1384
01:33:47.557 --> 01:33:49.072
Kafka uses KRaft.

1385
01:33:50.422 --> 01:33:51.659
Question 19.

1386
01:33:51.939 --> 01:33:54.499
Where would you deliberately avoid coordination?

1387
01:33:54.779 --> 01:33:56.145
Thirty-second answer:

1388
01:33:56.425 --> 01:34:12.553
Anywhere the invariant doesn't need it: carts (CRDT merge), counters and likes (sharded counters), analytics (eventual), notifications (idempotent, at-least-once), and partitioned work where each partition has one owner (Kafka consumer groups).

1389
01:34:12.833 --> 01:34:15.499
Coordinate for money, uniqueness and leadership.

1390
01:34:16.849 --> 01:34:17.959
Question 20.

1391
01:34:18.239 --> 01:34:21.737
A Redis-lock-based job ran twice and paid stores twice.

1392
01:34:22.017 --> 01:34:23.681
Run the post-incident review.

1393
01:34:23.961 --> 01:34:25.327
Thirty-second answer:

1394
01:34:25.607 --> 01:34:34.993
Root cause: a lock with a TTL and no fencing, where a GC or VM pause outlived the TTL, plus direct side effects from the leader.

1395
01:34:35.273 --> 01:34:49.289
Fixes: a consensus-backed election, fencing tokens enforced by the settlement table, bank file generation by an idempotent dispatcher keyed on (store, date), reconciliation before release, and pause monitoring.

1396
01:34:50.639 --> 01:34:51.535
Part 9.

1397
01:34:51.815 --> 01:34:53.223
Summary and Practice.

1398
01:34:54.473 --> 01:34:55.561
Key takeaways.

1399
01:34:55.841 --> 01:34:57.655
Consistency has three meanings.

1400
01:34:57.935 --> 01:35:03.695
In CAP and replication it means linearizability: one copy, real-time order.

1401
01:35:03.975 --> 01:35:10.375
The ladder: strict serializable, then linearizable, then sequential, then causal, then session, then eventual.

1402
01:35:10.655 --> 01:35:12.681
Pick per data class and operation.

1403
01:35:12.961 --> 01:35:20.471
CAP is about what happens during a partition, and PACELC adds latency versus consistency the rest of the time.

1404
01:35:20.751 --> 01:35:22.095
Clocks drift and jump.

1405
01:35:22.375 --> 01:35:32.423
Never order cross-machine events by wall-clock timestamps, and never measure durations with wall clocks (L1: minus 3,800 ms elapsed).

1406
01:35:33.773 --> 01:35:41.645
Lamport for causal total order, vector for detecting concurrency, HLC for causal and human-readable timestamps.

1407
01:35:41.925 --> 01:35:44.826
Consensus = majority agreement on a log.

1408
01:35:45.106 --> 01:35:51.143
2f + 1 nodes tolerate f failures, it's always safe, and live when the network behaves.

1409
01:35:51.423 --> 01:35:59.551
Raft's two critical rules, the up-to-date vote check and committing only current-term entries, are each shown in L3 to be necessary.

1410
01:35:59.831 --> 01:36:06.658
Reads need care: leader-local reads are stale after deposition (66,224).

1411
01:36:06.938 --> 01:36:08.239
ReadIndex is safe.

1412
01:36:08.519 --> 01:36:11.378
Leases are safe only within the clock-drift bound.

1413
01:36:12.728 --> 01:36:14.819
Leases don't stop paused processes.

1414
01:36:15.099 --> 01:36:20.240
Fencing tokens enforced by the resource do (lab: stale write, then 0 rows).

1415
01:36:20.520 --> 01:36:25.171
Coordinate only where invariants demand it: money, uniqueness, leadership.

1416
01:36:25.451 --> 01:36:28.693
Merge, partition or make everything else idempotent.

1417
